          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Copper coats the nail; the blue solution fades <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Copper displaces the iron from the nail <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">The nail dissolves and hydrogen gas fizzes off <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q4"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 RUSTING TEACH -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="rust">
      <span class="tag lo">Rusting &amp; redox</span>
      <h2>Rusting of iron &amp; its prevention</h2>
      <p>Iron rusts only when <b>both water and oxygen</b> are present (salt speeds it up). Rusting is an <b>oxidation</b> — the iron gains oxygen. We prevent it by keeping water and oxygen out, or by using a more reactive metal:</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Three ways to prevent rusting">
          <g font-family="Fredoka" text-anchor="middle">
            <rect x="20" y="30" width="160" height="90" rx="14" fill="#eef6ff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
            <text x="100" y="58" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">Barrier</text>
            <text x="100" y="82" font-size="11.5" fill="#0b5bbf">paint, oil, plastic</text>
            <text x="100" y="100" font-size="11.5" fill="#0b5bbf">keeps water/air out</text>
            <rect x="200" y="30" width="160" height="90" rx="14" fill="#eafaf0" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
            <text x="280" y="58" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">Galvanising</text>
            <text x="280" y="82" font-size="11.5" fill="#137a45">coat with zinc —</text>
            <text x="280" y="100" font-size="11.5" fill="#137a45">barrier + sacrificial</text>
            <rect x="380" y="30" width="160" height="90" rx="14" fill="#fff1c2" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
            <text x="460" y="58" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#8b6a00">Sacrificial</text>
            <text x="460" y="82" font-size="11.5" fill="#8b6a00">block of Mg/Zn</text>
            <text x="460" y="100" font-size="11.5" fill="#8b6a00">corrodes instead</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Sacrificial protection uses a <b>more reactive</b> metal (Zn, Mg) that oxidises in place of the iron.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="note"><b>Redox in a line:</b> <b>OIL RIG</b> — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons). The thing oxidised is the <b>reducing agent</b>; the thing reduced is the <b>oxidising agent</b>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 10 EXTRACTION TEACH -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="extract">
      <span class="tag lo">Extraction of metals</span>
      <h2>How position decides the method</h2>
      <p>Most metals are dug up as <b>ores</b> (compounds). Unreactive metals like gold are found <b>native</b> (uncombined). The <b>method of extraction depends on reactivity (2.23C)</b>:</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 170" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Aluminium by electrolysis, iron by reduction with carbon">
          <rect x="20" y="22" width="250" height="126" rx="16" fill="#efe1ff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="145" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#6a30b8">More reactive than carbon</text>
          <text x="145" y="74" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">→ electrolysis</text>
          <text x="145" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12.5" fill="#6a30b8">Aluminium from</text>
          <text x="145" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12.5" fill="#6a30b8">molten aluminium oxide</text>
          <text x="145" y="138" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">(carbon can't pull O off Al)</text>
          <rect x="290" y="22" width="250" height="126" rx="16" fill="#d6f6e4" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="415" y="46" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#137a45">Less reactive than carbon</text>
          <text x="415" y="74" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">→ reduce with carbon</text>
          <text x="415" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12.5" fill="#137a45">Iron from iron oxide:</text>
          <text x="415" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12" fill="#137a45">2Fe₂O₃ + 3C → 4Fe + 3CO₂</text>
          <text x="415" y="138" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">carbon is cheaper than electricity</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Metals <b>above</b> carbon need electrolysis; metals <b>below</b> carbon can be reduced by carbon.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>The 4CH1 spec uses <b>iron (carbon reduction)</b> and <b>aluminium (electrolysis)</b> to illustrate this link — detailed process steps are <b>not</b> required, but you should comment on a process when given information.</p>
      <p class="note"><b>Uses (2.25C):</b> aluminium — low density, corrosion-resistant; copper — good conductor; iron/steel — strong and cheap. An <b>alloy</b> (e.g. steel) is harder than a pure metal because different-sized atoms <b>disrupt the layers</b> so they can't slide.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 11 EXTRACTION TEST -->
    <section class="screen" data-test="extract">
      <span class="tag">Choose the method</span>
      <h2>How would you extract it?</h2>
      <div class="activity">
